 { Chessable note: This line from an actual game highlights a series of mistakes from both players, and hence cannot be memorised. Instead we've split it into a few useful training exercises that follow this informational line. The series of checksLariño - Picazo, la Roda, 2006 This position comes from an actual game. Once again, we can see that, although White's position looks desperate because the king is far away from the promotion squares, there are plenty of resources for the rook. In concrete, this example shows the effectiveness of a series of checks to clear the way for the 'tail-hook'. } 1... g3 ( 1... h4 { the other logical advance, leads to a draw as well: A. } 2. Rg7 { Bringing the king closer also leads to a draw, but this move - less intuitive in appearance - reveals the idea in a clearer way: the king approaches through the g-and h-files until he makes contact with the backward pawn. } Kf4 3. Rf7 Kg3 4. Kg7 h3 5. Kg6 $1 { - it is important to leave the h-file open in case Rh7 is needed - } Kh2 ( 5... h2 6. Rh7 Kg2 7. Kg5 g3 8. Kg4 $11 ) 
6. Kg5 g3 7. Kh4 g2 8. Rg7 $11 { B. } ( 1... h4 2. Ke7 h3 3. Ke6 Kf4 4. Kd5 Kg3 ( 4... g3 5. Rh4 $11 ) 
5. Ke4 Kh2 6. Rg7 g3 7. Kf3 g2 8. Kf2 Kh1 9. Rg6 h2 10. Rxg2 $11 ) 
 ) 
2. Ke7 { It is logical to bring the king closer, but again it is clearer to start by delivering rook checks: } ( 2. Rg7 Kf4 3. Rf7 Kg4 4. Rg7 Kf3 ( 4. Ke7 Kh3 5. Kf7 g2 6. Rg5 $1 h4 7. Kg6 Kh2 8. Kh5 h3 9. Kh4 ) 
5. Rf7 Kg2 6. Rh7 ) 
h4 3. Ke6 Kg4 4. Ke5 $2 { White plays a logical move (to bring the king closer) ... and reaches a losing position. It was necessary to switch to the other plan: the rook delivers checks and the king tries to get in contact with the backward pawn. } ( 4. Rg7 Kf3 ( 4. Ke5 Kf4 5. Rh7 g2 6. Rxh4 $11 ) 
 ( 4. Ke5 Kh3 5. Kf5 Kh2 6. Kg4 $11 ) 
5. Rf7 Kg2 ( 5. Ke5 Ke3 6. Rg7 $1 { hindering the pawns' advance: } Kf2 7. Rf7 Kg1 8. Rh7 $1 ) 
6. Kf5 h3 7. Kg4 { (the king contacts the pawn: draw) } h2 8. Rh7 Kf2 9. Kf4 $11 ) 
g2 { Returning the favour right away. Calculations are very complex here. Placing both pawns on the 6th rank was winning, though there were some mating traps to avoid: } ( 4... h3 $1 5. Ke4 ( 4... g2 4. Rg7 Kf3 6. Rf7 Ke2 $1 { and the pawns promote without aid } ) 
h2 6. Rh8 ( 5... g2 5. Rg7 Kh5 $1 7. Kf4 g2 $19 ) 
g2 7. Rg8 Kh5 $1 { the king flees from mate; } ( 7... Kh3 $2 8. Kf3 g1=N { != } ) 
8. Kf5 Kh6 9. Kf6 Kh7 $19 ) 
5. Ke4 Kh3 6. Rg7 Kh2 7. Kf4 $2 ( 7. Kf3 $1 h3 8. Kf2 Kh1 $11 ) 
h3 8. Kf3 g1=Q 
